# SET-PIECE: the chokepoint.
#
# A grave in an open bay, with a three-cell gate in a wall between it and the way
# you came. That is the whole composition, and it states the pack's question in
# geometry: a rusher is answered by MOVING, and a pack cannot be, because sideways
# is where the next one is. A pack is answered by GROUND.
#
# THE GATE IS BEHIND YOU, NOT AHEAD. You walk through it, the grave opens three
# cells later, and the good ground is the ground you have just left -- so the
# decision is whether to give up the metres you spent. Placed ahead it would be a
# race rather than a choice, and a race has one right answer.
#
# The bay is DELIBERATELY EMPTY. The dressing pass never writes into a piece's
# rows (`seg.piece_rows`), so nothing will fill it in later, and that is the
# point: a second answer in the middle of the bay would let the party solve this
# without ever noticing the gate. A composition that can be ignored is texture.
#
# WHY A WALL AND NOT A GAP. `X` is Kind.WALL -- solid, unwalkable, and a sight
# blocker on layer 1 -- and the sight test is the mechanism this piece is really
# about. A zombie that cannot see anybody has no target and stands still, so the
# wall does not merely narrow the frontage, it switches off the half of the pack
# that has not come round yet. Holes would have narrowed the frontage too and
# added a way to die to the answer, which is a different lesson.
#
# THE GRENADE IS PAST THE GRAVE, and it is the rusher pit's joke told again on
# purpose: a blast is what EMPTIES a grave, so the tool that would have deleted
# this encounter is what you get for having survived it. It is a pre-empt for the
# next one, which is the only way a consumable is ever allowed to matter (2a-i).

name = piece_zombie_choke
base_height = 0
width = 21
length = 7
piece_exit = 0
tags = foot, piece, horde

